As deep neural networks deliver superior performance in image recognition tasks, relying on them for high-stakes decisions has become more commonplace. However, the critical difference between human-made functions and neural networks lies in their explicit inclusion of visual invariances, which underscores the necessity to collaborate with human experts to refine models and mitigate inconsistencies. By combining the strengths of both parties, we develop a multi-agent system that amplifies authentic data samples rated high in trustworthiness and reduces confidence gaps between models. Our proposed method, tested on the MNIST dataset with variations, demonstrates its ability to enhance model performance and reliability. Interestingly, our analysis reveals that incorporating diverse datasets enhances the stability of models selected for refinement. Ultimately, our multi-agent system successfully minimizes the discrepancies among models and achieves comparable results to techniques like neural torture and data augmentation, but with a key difference - it explicitly considers the trust ratings of human experts. Overall, our approach provides a pioneering solution for addressing issues of over-confidence and inconsistency in neural networks. Additionally, we explore the application of our method to classification tasks and observe similar results, leading us to contemplate its potential to tackle other challenges in deep learning, such as class imbalance and gradient skewness. Our code is publicly available on GitHub at https://github.com/ssecunderland/Highway-Renewal. 

